<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Heraclea Lucania</h1><p class="st-meta">433 BCE – 278 BCE (approx.) · capital Heraclea</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek city founded jointly by Taras and Thurii in 433 BCE, seat of the Italiote League and site of Pyrrhus's first victory over Rome.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taras">Taras</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thurii">Thurii</a> fought over the territory of ruined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/siris">Siris</a>, they settled the dispute by founding a new city together in 433 or 432 BCE, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a> taking the lead. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perinthus">Heraclea</a> was built on a plateau near the Siris and Aciris rivers, at modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/policoro">Policoro</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perinthus">Heraclea</a> was dominated by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taras">Taras</a>, which moved the meeting place of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/italiote-league">Italiote League</a> there in the 4th century BCE, and it held the league's festival. The city prospered from farming the coastal plain. The bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tables-of-heraclea">Tables of Heraclea</a>, found in 1732, record on one side Greek temple land surveys and on the other a Roman municipal law of the 1st century BCE.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 280 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-valerius-laevinus">Laevinus</a> at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/perinthus">Heraclea</a>, using elephants. In 278 BCE Heraclea made a treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> on very favourable terms. It became a Roman municipium after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> and declined in late antiquity.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/heraclea-lucania.jpg" alt="Heraclea Lucania" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Alessandro Antonelli · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Area_archeologica_di_Herakleia_e_Siris_-_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>433 BCE – 278 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Heraclea</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Herakleia, Heraclea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-356&amp;focus=heraclea-lucania" aria-label="Heraclea Lucania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5090 740 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Heraclea Lucania lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5410 996L5424 988M5410 996L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5424 988h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5410 996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5410 996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-356&amp;focus=heraclea-lucania">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
